The database has three tables: items, one row per enqueued article; recipe_versions, one row per version of a domain's recipe; and cost_entries, one row per metered call. The diagram shows only the fields this design adds. The shapes of the item row, the unit list and the item Workflow's params and output are TypeScript types in common/, which both Workers import.

An item row keeps its unit list and its degradations as JSON columns; a long article's units come to a few hundred kilobytes, far below D1's 10 MB limit for one value. workflow_instance_id names the item's Workflow instance, and updated_at records the last write to the row. While the Workflow runs, the item's status and progress are read from the instance, and status on the row is empty. Once the Workflow finishes, the row holds the finished state: status, ready or failed, the title, the audio duration and format, and the error of a failed item. From then on the instance is not read, so an item stays readable after Cloudflare drops the instance at the end of its retention window. The row has no fields for an attempt in progress.

A ledger row holds the event type, the raw measure and its unit, the dollar cost at write time and an optional JSON detail. cached is true when AI Gateway's response cache answered the call and false otherwise. A describe answered from the cache writes a row at the same flat price as one that reached Gemini, and cached tells the two apart.

